{% decision_card number="ADR-0016" status="approved" %}
One decision. Status reviewed 2026-08-08.
{% /decision_card %}

## Context

Beacon-Co's eight-subagent build (ADR-0008) needs a routing/cost layer
ahead of the other seven roles: something to estimate prompt cost, tighten
context, and choose between Claude and the second provider actually
available on this machine (Codex CLI, confirmed installed and
authenticated). No token-optimization system exists yet in this repo —
there is no knowledge-compiler, retrieval layer, or context-budgeter to
build against or narrow down from. This decision is standalone, not a
slice of a larger prior design.

## Decision drivers

- avoid re-pasting context that already exists as a repo file;
- keep routing between Claude and Codex explicit and auditable, not
  vibes-based;
- avoid building infrastructure (embeddings, vector store, RAG, a
  fine-tuned router) this solo-operator, pre-revenue repo does not need
  yet;
- produce local, logged evidence before any larger claim about savings is
  made.

## Options considered

- no prompt-optimization step at all — every agent invocation carries
  full, unfiltered context;
- a full token-optimization system (compiler, index, embeddings, semantic
  routing) built up front;
- a narrow, rule-based pilot: estimate, compress via five ordered
  extractive tactics, route via explicit rules, log to a plain file.

## Decision

Build the narrow pilot. `token-auditor` (Read/Glob/Grep only,
`permissionMode: plan`) estimates token cost, applies reference-first and
extractive-only compression (never paraphrase-compressing code, paths,
SHAs, or exact error text), and routes to Claude by default or to Codex
CLI when ADR-0009's independent-second-voice rationale applies. It does
not persist its own log — no `Write`/`Edit`/`Bash` — it returns the log
line as text for the invoking context to append to
`.beacon/telemetry/token-audit.log`.

This continues ADR-0004's still-valid part (repository-first,
file-based memory over an early broker) applied to exactly one narrow
job, and operationalizes ADR-0008's "assign per work unit using policy"
for the one deliverable this role actually has: a routing decision with a
stated reason.

## Positive consequences

- every agent invocation can be tightened before it spends context;
- Claude/Codex routing decisions are explicit and logged, not silent;
- the log itself becomes the evidence base for later deciding whether to
  expand, keep flat, or drop this role;
- no new infrastructure, no database, no embeddings — nothing to
  maintain beyond one subagent file and one log file.

## Negative consequences

- an extra step ahead of every other agent invocation adds latency;
- rule-based routing will misclassify some tasks until the rules are
  tuned against real logged outcomes;
- `haiku` (chosen for cost/frequency fit) may under-judge ambiguous
  compression calls that a larger model would catch — acceptable for a
  pilot, not yet proven for scale.

## Risks

- log entries could go unwritten if the invoking context forgets to
  persist the returned line — there is no automatic enforcement of this
  handoff;
- routing rules could harden into unsupported preference the same way
  ADR-0008 already flags as a risk for the broader role/provider system;
- without real logged data yet, any early savings claim would be
  unsupported — none is made here.

## Follow-up work

- accumulate real entries in `.beacon/telemetry/token-audit.log` before
  claiming any savings percentage;
- revisit `haiku` vs. a larger model once misrouted or under-compressed
  cases show up in the log;
- do not build embeddings/RAG/a fine-tuned router until this pilot's own
  data shows a specific, recurring gap it cannot cover.

## Supersedes or superseded by

New decision; superseded by: none. Not a slice of any prior ADR — none
existed to slice from.

